--TEST--
Inside a declaration, `self::`, `parent::` and `static::` each resolve at the four sites a class side is asked for, with `self`/`parent` fixed where they are written and `static` following the class the call was made on — the accepting half of the bound the reject case beside this one draws, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/refused-in-expression-position
// The other side of the bound: inside a declaration the same four spellings
// each resolve, so what the refusal is about is where they are written and
// not the spelling itself. `self::` and `parent::` are fixed where they
// stand, and `static::` follows the class the call was made on.
class Base {
    public const string LABEL = "base";

    public static int $made = 0;

    public static function name(): string {
        return "Base";
    }

    public static function make(): static {
        self::$made = self::$made + 1;
        return new static();
    }
}

final class Child extends Base {
    public const string LABEL = "child";

    public static function name(): string {
        return "Child";
    }

    public static function written(): string {
        return self::LABEL . " over " . parent::LABEL;
    }

    public static function called(): string {
        return static::name();
    }
}

echo Child::written(), "\n";
echo Child::called(), "\n";
var $made = Child::make();
echo $made is Child ? "a Child" : "not a Child", "\n";
echo Base::$made, "\n";
--EXPECT--
child over base
Child
a Child
1
